00:10:26,340 --> 00:10:29,380
All right, now we're going to start with game theory.

269
00:10:29,380 --> 00:10:31,060
All right, so how many people here

270
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:33,140
have done game theory at some point in your life?

271
00:10:33,140 --> 00:10:33,820
All right, good.

272
00:10:33,820 --> 00:10:35,900
All right, so a lot of this will be review.

273
00:10:35,900 --> 00:10:38,020
Hopefully, we will take it to a higher level.

274
00:10:38,020 --> 00:10:39,700
And yet, I will not torture you with math

275
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:41,240
that you'll never need to know again.

276
00:10:41,240 --> 00:10:43,620
I'm going to try to give you enough math

277
00:10:43,620 --> 00:10:47,060
so that you understand the basics and can read papers,

278
00:10:47,060 --> 00:10:49,540
and then also give you enough additional information

279
00:10:49,540 --> 00:10:52,260
that you could write papers and do research.

280
00:10:52,260 --> 00:10:54,100
So we'll see.

281
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:55,900
All right.

282
00:10:55,900 --> 00:11:00,300
So as I said, with general equilibrium theory,

283
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:02,020
you can go pretty far.

284
00:11:02,020 --> 00:11:05,180
So we can analyze an economy with any number of people,

285
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:07,820
any number of goods, any number of time periods,

286
00:11:07,820 --> 00:11:10,100
any number of states of affairs.

287
00:11:10,100 --> 00:11:13,100
So that sounds like we've got a pretty big set

288
00:11:13,100 --> 00:11:14,940
of possibilities.

289
00:11:14,940 --> 00:11:17,900
But in the end, most people who go explore general equilibrium

290
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:20,820
long enough decide that there's too many questions that it

291
00:11:20,820 --> 00:11:22,020
leaves unanswered.

292
00:11:22,020 --> 00:11:24,220
There is a well-known book by Fisher Black,

293
00:11:24,220 --> 00:11:26,340
you may have heard of him, called Exploring General

294
00:11:26,340 --> 00:11:27,020
Equilibrium.

295
00:11:27,020 --> 00:11:30,420
And this is what's called an idiosyncratic book.

296
00:11:30,420 --> 00:11:33,500
This is a book where a famous economist just says, look,

297
00:11:33,500 --> 00:11:35,700
I'm going to explain everything in the whole universe

298
00:11:35,700 --> 00:11:37,940
using general equilibrium theory.

299
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:40,180
Try to stop me.

300
00:11:40,180 --> 00:11:42,420
And it's the kind of book you're like, well,

301
00:11:42,420 --> 00:11:43,660
it's a very interesting book.

302
00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:47,020
And it's a powerful intellect behind it,

303
00:11:47,020 --> 00:11:50,100
somewhat dogmatic or maybe incredibly dogmatic.

304
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:52,900
But still, there are a lot of people who read it

305
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:55,860
and just kind of liked it.

306
00:11:55,860 --> 00:11:56,540
It's different.

307
00:11:56,540 --> 00:11:57,660
Anyway.

308
00:11:57,660 --> 00:12:00,460
But well, let's take a regret in the Fisher Black's world

309
00:12:00,460 --> 00:12:01,940
and move on.

310
00:12:01,940 --> 00:12:05,540
So what most people find general equilibrium very unsatisfying.

311
00:12:05,540 --> 00:12:07,620
And because in the real world, people usually

312
00:12:07,620 --> 00:12:10,740
stand somewhere in between a simple one-agent world,

313
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:13,780
where it's like me hunting crawfish, where I figure out

314
00:12:13,780 --> 00:12:15,540
what the crawfish do, and then I eat them,

315
00:12:15,540 --> 00:12:17,700
and the crawfish keep being idiots.

316
00:12:17,700 --> 00:12:19,020
So that's one world.

317
00:12:19,020 --> 00:12:21,020
And the other world is the infinite agent world,

318
00:12:21,020 --> 00:12:24,140
or the near infinite agent world that we've been talking about.

319
00:12:24,140 --> 00:12:26,540
And that's another one.

320
00:12:26,540 --> 00:12:28,820
And then furthermore, even when you start out

321
00:12:28,820 --> 00:12:32,980
in this near infinite agent case, a lot of times in the end,

322
00:12:32,980 --> 00:12:34,620
things get narrowed down.

323
00:12:34,620 --> 00:12:36,700
So think about what life is like, first of all,

324
00:12:36,700 --> 00:12:38,420
for a single person.

325
00:12:38,420 --> 00:12:41,060
So even easier in the world of online dating.

326
00:12:41,060 --> 00:12:45,820
So how many people are available for online dating to you?

327
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:48,060
Tens of millions of people.

328
00:12:48,060 --> 00:12:51,140
So it seems like general equilibrium is about right.

329
00:12:51,140 --> 00:12:53,820
And I don't think anyone is saying, well, look,

330
00:12:53,820 --> 00:12:56,300
if I'm a bad date, this might lead

331
00:12:56,300 --> 00:12:59,700
to a general decline in the quality of my opportunities.

332
00:12:59,700 --> 00:13:02,020
So I'd better be very careful here,

333
00:13:02,020 --> 00:13:05,500
lest I unseat the social balance.

334
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:08,020
So probably no one, when they're doing online dating,

335
00:13:08,020 --> 00:13:10,100
is worrying that they personally are

336
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:12,780
going to change the way that the world of dating works.

337
00:13:12,780 --> 00:13:15,140
No one says, look, if I'm really nice,

