31
00:21:15,480 --> 00:21:16,880
It's worked that way.

32
00:21:16,880 --> 00:21:17,880
OK.

33
00:21:17,880 --> 00:21:18,880
All right.

34
00:21:18,880 --> 00:21:19,380
OK.

35
00:21:19,380 --> 00:21:20,600
So we have a simple example.

36
00:21:20,600 --> 00:21:23,040
A more interesting one would be something

37
00:21:23,040 --> 00:21:25,480
based upon an old and pretty boring movie called

38
00:21:25,480 --> 00:21:28,000
Trench Connection, Gene Hackman in the 70s.

39
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:28,880
You might like it.

40
00:21:28,880 --> 00:21:29,460
I don't know.

41
00:21:29,460 --> 00:21:30,760
It's one I saw as an adult.

42
00:21:30,760 --> 00:21:32,260
This is what they watched back then.

43
00:21:32,260 --> 00:21:33,880
It's a good job.

44
00:21:33,880 --> 00:21:38,240
Anyway, so there's a cop who's strangely named Popeye.

45
00:21:38,240 --> 00:21:39,200
And I don't know why.

46
00:21:39,200 --> 00:21:40,520
He's not a cartoon character.

47
00:21:40,520 --> 00:21:42,640
He does not like the spinach-eating cartoon

48
00:21:42,640 --> 00:21:44,200
character.

49
00:21:44,200 --> 00:21:46,000
Both his eyes are fine.

50
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:47,680
But anyway, he's called Popeye.

51
00:21:47,680 --> 00:21:51,800
And he is pursuing a criminal onto a New York subway.

52
00:21:51,800 --> 00:21:55,360
And when this criminal notices Popeye,

53
00:21:55,360 --> 00:21:57,120
Popeye notices the criminal.

54
00:21:57,120 --> 00:21:59,560
And then they're trying to figure out what to do.

55
00:21:59,560 --> 00:22:01,760
Because Popeye, in order to follow the criminal,

56
00:22:01,760 --> 00:22:04,280
needs to do the same thing as the criminal.

57
00:22:04,280 --> 00:22:06,920
If the criminal's on the subway, Popeye must be on the subway.

58
00:22:06,920 --> 00:22:08,400
If the criminal's not on the subway,

59
00:22:08,400 --> 00:22:10,520
Popeye must not be on the subway.

60
00:22:10,520 --> 00:22:12,080
On the other hand, the criminal wants

61
00:22:12,080 --> 00:22:14,360
to do the opposite of what the cop who's following him

62
00:22:14,360 --> 00:22:15,000
is doing.

63
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:18,840
If the cop is on the subway, the criminal wants to get off.

64
00:22:18,840 --> 00:22:20,600
And if the cop is not on the subway,

65
00:22:20,600 --> 00:22:23,560
the criminal wants to be on.

66
00:22:23,560 --> 00:22:26,880
So what you could do is start off with, first of all,

67
00:22:26,880 --> 00:22:28,520
the criminal, player one, decides

68
00:22:28,520 --> 00:22:30,400
whether to be on or off.

69
00:22:30,400 --> 00:22:31,880
And then Popeye comes along.

70
00:22:31,880 --> 00:22:34,240
And he decides whether he wants to be on or off.

71
00:22:38,680 --> 00:22:41,800
And then we could go and list the following payoffs.

72
00:22:41,800 --> 00:22:44,360
So say that if you get what you want,

73
00:22:44,360 --> 00:22:45,840
then you have a payoff of 10.

74
00:22:45,840 --> 00:22:47,640
If you don't, you have a payoff of 0.

75
00:22:47,640 --> 00:22:49,720
So if they're both on, then the criminal

76
00:22:49,720 --> 00:22:50,880
went first as a payoff of 0.

77
00:22:50,880 --> 00:22:53,120
And Popeye is 10.

78
00:22:53,120 --> 00:22:57,080
And if the criminal is on and Popeye is off,

79
00:22:57,080 --> 00:22:58,360
then the criminal gets 10.

80
00:22:58,360 --> 00:22:59,760
Popeye gets 0.

81
00:22:59,760 --> 00:23:02,280
If it's off-on, then you get 10, 0.

82
00:23:02,280 --> 00:23:05,240
Then finally, off-off would be 0, 10.

83
00:23:05,240 --> 00:23:10,440
And that is the extensive form for that game.

84
00:23:10,440 --> 00:23:12,480
Now, there's a few different complications

85
00:23:12,480 --> 00:23:15,240
that get worked into this approach.

86
00:23:15,240 --> 00:23:18,680
So one of them is, what if nature is playing?

87
00:23:18,680 --> 00:23:19,840
What if nature is playing?

88
00:23:19,840 --> 00:23:23,800
What if it can either be icy or not be icy?

89
00:23:23,800 --> 00:23:25,720
And the normal way of doing this is actually

90
00:23:25,720 --> 00:23:28,800
to just insert nature into the game

91
00:23:28,800 --> 00:23:32,240
as a player who randomizes.

92
00:23:32,240 --> 00:23:33,440
And why are they randomized?

93
00:23:33,440 --> 00:23:35,120
Well, intuitively, you just think nature

94
00:23:35,120 --> 00:23:37,040
gets the same payoff regardless.

95
00:23:37,040 --> 00:23:39,720
Because nature isn't different to our existence.

96
00:23:39,720 --> 00:23:42,280
Nature does not care about human affairs.

97
00:23:42,280 --> 00:23:44,200
And therefore, nature gets modeled

98
00:23:44,200 --> 00:23:47,840
as a player that acts randomly.

99
00:23:47,840 --> 00:23:51,840
So there are a lot of models where someone takes an action,

100
00:23:51,840 --> 00:23:54,840
and then nature plays.

101
00:23:54,840 --> 00:23:57,520
Nature could be, for example, there's either a hard earther,

102
00:23:57,520 --> 00:23:59,280
there's rainstorms or not.

103
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:01,600
And then this determines whether the farmer invested well

104
00:24:01,600 --> 00:24:02,100
or poorly.

105
00:24:04,520 --> 00:24:06,440
Now, another thing you may be wondering about

106
00:24:06,440 --> 00:24:09,000
is, how does information work in this game?

107
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:13,600
And the key insight here is that when you really think about it,

108
00:24:13,600 --> 00:24:18,280
there's no difference at all between moving simultaneously

109
00:24:18,280 --> 00:24:20,760
and moving sequentially but not knowing what

110
00:24:20,760 --> 00:24:23,120
your opponent did first.

111
00:24:23,120 --> 00:24:26,000
So for example, suppose that we could play rock, paper, scissors.

112
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:29,040
We either do it simultaneously, which is the usual way,

113
00:24:29,040 --> 00:24:32,360
or I could go and write what I'm doing on a piece of paper

114
00:24:32,360 --> 00:24:36,440
secretly, put it down, walk away so I can't touch it anymore,

115
00:24:36,440 --> 00:24:39,120
and then you write down what you're going to do secretly

116
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:42,360
so you don't get to see what I did

117
00:24:42,360 --> 00:24:45,720
and I can't change what I do, and then we open them.

118
00:24:45,720 --> 00:24:48,560
And what's the difference between doing it simultaneously

119
00:24:48,560 --> 00:24:51,160
with full information or doing it sequentially

120
00:24:51,160 --> 00:24:52,520
without the information?

121
00:24:52,520 --> 00:24:54,880
The answer is it's exactly the same thing.

122
00:24:54,880 --> 00:24:57,840
And that's nice because to do it as a tree,

123
00:24:57,840 --> 00:25:00,320
everything needs to be sequential.

124
00:25:00,320 --> 00:25:05,200
So anyway, we can go and model this,

125
00:25:05,200 --> 00:25:08,480
what appears to be simultaneity here.

126
00:25:08,480 --> 00:25:10,120
And we can make everything sequential.

127
00:25:10,120 --> 00:25:15,680
And what we do is that if, say, let's see,

128
00:25:15,680 --> 00:25:20,160
if this player doesn't get to see what happened there,

129
00:25:20,160 --> 00:25:22,640
then we just brought dots around that first thing.

130
00:25:30,080 --> 00:25:32,000
So basically, you put dots around there,

131
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,120
and that shows that when player two is deciding,

132
00:25:34,120 --> 00:25:36,920
he doesn't know where he is in the tree.

133
00:25:36,920 --> 00:25:39,920
So we're still thinking of it as sequential,

134
00:25:39,920 --> 00:25:41,360
and then you might say, well, yeah,

135
00:25:41,360 --> 00:25:43,880
couldn't we just as well flip it around and change who player

136
00:25:43,880 --> 00:25:44,880
one and player two are?

137
00:25:44,880 --> 00:25:45,760
Yeah, that's true.

138
00:25:45,760 --> 00:25:48,400
But still, this means that we can go and model

139
00:25:48,400 --> 00:25:50,600
this in this nice tree shape.

140
00:25:53,480 --> 00:25:55,760
So if encyclopedias moves are equivalent sequentially,

141
00:25:55,760 --> 00:25:57,880
we're presenting potential moves with uncertainty.

142
00:25:57,880 --> 00:25:59,300
And then, again, the whole idea is

143
00:25:59,300 --> 00:26:01,440
if you learn something before you decide,

144
00:26:01,440 --> 00:26:04,600
then the node representing what's learned must precede

145
00:26:04,600 --> 00:26:08,320
the node the decision is taken.

146
00:26:08,320 --> 00:26:10,000
So this is extensive forms.

147
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,720
Extensive forms are mainly used for very simple games

148
00:26:12,720 --> 00:26:14,880
because it's really hard to draw this

149
00:26:14,880 --> 00:26:16,600
when the game is really big.

150
00:26:16,600 --> 00:26:19,000
And it's very hard to draw it if there's a lot of players.

151
00:26:21,560 --> 00:26:23,840
But there are people who've gotten Nobel prizes

152
00:26:23,840 --> 00:26:25,360
for pretty simple models.

153
00:26:25,360 --> 00:26:29,920
So this can be done, say, on Reinhardt's Elm

154
00:26:29,920 --> 00:26:31,240
with the chain store model.

155
00:26:31,240 --> 00:26:34,480
That's a fairly simple model, but barely more complicated

156
00:26:34,480 --> 00:26:37,280
than this.

157
00:26:37,280 --> 00:26:39,960
Questions about extensive forms?

158
00:26:39,960 --> 00:26:41,440
Have you assessed forms yet?

159
00:26:41,440 --> 00:26:42,920
Under what conditions can we assume

160
00:26:42,920 --> 00:26:45,880
that everybody knows all the payoffs,

161
00:26:45,880 --> 00:26:48,280
no matter what decisions are made after their decisions?

162
00:26:48,280 --> 00:26:49,760
So that's a great question.

163
00:26:49,760 --> 00:26:52,080
And the game theory answer is, well,

164
00:26:52,080 --> 00:26:53,520
if people don't know the payoffs,

165
00:26:53,520 --> 00:26:55,120
then we just have to add more levels

166
00:26:55,120 --> 00:26:57,080
of complexity to the game.

167
00:26:57,080 --> 00:26:58,520
You say, man, but people can't even

168
00:26:58,520 --> 00:27:01,560
understand the simple version of the game.

169
00:27:01,560 --> 00:27:03,840
So that is the idea, is that if people don't know what

170
00:27:03,840 --> 00:27:06,600
the payoffs are, then you just add an extra level saying,

171
00:27:06,600 --> 00:27:07,920
well, then they think the payoffs

172
00:27:07,920 --> 00:27:09,760
could be a bunch of different possibilities.

173
00:27:09,760 --> 00:27:11,040
We just have to make it bigger.

174
00:27:11,040 --> 00:27:12,800
So that's how you would conventionally

175
00:27:12,800 --> 00:27:14,800
model any kind of confusion.

176
00:27:14,800 --> 00:27:17,400
And actually, there is a whole set of models

177
00:27:17,400 --> 00:27:19,480
that people use on the idea, well,

178
00:27:19,480 --> 00:27:21,600
maybe people are cooperating with Persona of Silence

179
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:23,880
because they don't understand the payoffs.

180
00:27:23,880 --> 00:27:25,680
And so that's very similar, actually,

181
00:27:25,680 --> 00:27:28,400
to that self-managed chain store paradox I was mentioning.

182
00:27:28,400 --> 00:27:32,800
So there are models like that, I guess.

183
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:37,240
So if you have a sequential game, and we let, for example,

184
00:27:37,240 --> 00:27:39,880
like the second player, the player two,

185
00:27:39,880 --> 00:27:43,640
assume that in each case the player one made,

186
00:27:43,640 --> 00:27:46,960
what the second player will choose, at the same time,

187
00:27:46,960 --> 00:27:49,640
is that still a sequential game or not?

188
00:27:49,640 --> 00:27:50,140
Let's see.

189
00:27:50,140 --> 00:27:52,600
So you're saying, so the second player

190
00:27:52,600 --> 00:27:54,320
does know what the first player did?

191
00:27:54,320 --> 00:27:56,440
He doesn't know, but he assumed that in this case,

192
00:27:56,440 --> 00:27:57,360
what he would choose.

193
00:27:57,360 --> 00:28:00,360
And if the player, if we assume that the first player chose

194
00:28:00,360 --> 00:28:02,080
on, then I would decide.

195
00:28:02,080 --> 00:28:03,880
So player two just makes an assumption

196
00:28:03,880 --> 00:28:05,240
about what the player one did?

197
00:28:05,240 --> 00:28:06,840
Yeah, they would.

198
00:28:06,840 --> 00:28:09,880
So I'd say, so then we're probably

199
00:28:09,880 --> 00:28:11,440
in the realm of psychology, which

200
00:28:11,440 --> 00:28:13,920
is great, and we'll talk about it after midterm.

201
00:28:13,920 --> 00:28:17,840
But if people make an assumption without any,

202
00:28:17,840 --> 00:28:20,400
people just assume that people are doing A,

203
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:21,920
when actually, some time, actually,

204
00:28:21,920 --> 00:28:23,520
they're not doing A 100% of the time,

205
00:28:23,520 --> 00:28:25,800
then that's a systematic bias.

206
00:28:25,800 --> 00:28:29,560
And then we need psychology to go and analyze that.

207
00:28:29,560 --> 00:28:32,760
I mean, assume that the first player chose on,

208
00:28:32,760 --> 00:28:35,720
then the second player chose on or off.

209
00:28:35,720 --> 00:28:38,320
And then assume that the first player chose off,

210
00:28:38,320 --> 00:28:40,240
then the second player chose on or off.

211
00:28:40,240 --> 00:28:42,120
And then they will reveal the true decision.

212
00:28:42,120 --> 00:28:45,080
OK, so that's when they don't know what the other one did.

213
00:28:45,080 --> 00:28:45,800
Yeah, they know.

214
00:28:45,800 --> 00:28:48,440
But if the real decision is revealed,

215
00:28:48,440 --> 00:28:53,320
they will pay by the real decision.

216
00:28:53,320 --> 00:28:55,400
Possibly, we're going to answer your question when

217
00:28:55,400 --> 00:28:56,600
we do an abstract equilibrium.

218
00:28:56,600 --> 00:28:58,520
How about after we do an abstract equilibrium,

219
00:28:58,520 --> 00:29:01,480
can you tell me if you're happy or if you're still?

220
00:29:01,480 --> 00:29:01,980
No.

221
00:29:01,980 --> 00:29:02,480
No.

222
00:29:02,480 --> 00:29:02,980
OK.

223
00:29:02,980 --> 00:29:03,720
Yes, that's fine.

224
00:29:03,720 --> 00:29:04,220
No, no, no.

225
00:29:04,220 --> 00:29:07,480
It's a good question.

226
00:29:07,480 --> 00:29:09,920
OK, so anyway, so since it gets hard

227
00:29:09,920 --> 00:29:12,360
to draw these extensive forms, and they're generally

228
00:29:12,360 --> 00:29:15,400
only used for simpler problems, although simple

229
00:29:15,400 --> 00:29:18,560
doesn't mean trivial, you can get a Nobel Prize

230
00:29:18,560 --> 00:29:20,560
for simple problems.

231
00:29:20,560 --> 00:29:24,280
You do it well, and a lot of people say you.

232
00:29:24,280 --> 00:29:26,920
But more commonly, we do normal forms,

233
00:29:26,920 --> 00:29:29,240
which are also called strategic forms.

234
00:29:29,240 --> 00:29:31,760
So what's going on with normal forms

235
00:29:31,760 --> 00:29:35,960
is you get a complete grid of strategy profiles and payoffs.

236
00:29:35,960 --> 00:29:38,440
And the grid is one dimension per player.

237
00:29:38,440 --> 00:29:39,920
Right now, of course, that's also

238
00:29:39,920 --> 00:29:40,880
getting a little complicated.

239
00:29:40,880 --> 00:29:41,760
What if there's four players?

240
00:29:41,760 --> 00:29:43,240
We need a four-dimensional diagram.

241
00:29:43,240 --> 00:29:45,480
But you may notice, wow, there's not that many theory

242
00:29:45,480 --> 00:29:48,760
papers with four players.

243
00:29:48,760 --> 00:29:51,480
Is that because the world never has four people interacting?

244
00:29:51,480 --> 00:29:55,080
Or is it because it's too hard to go and draw

245
00:29:55,080 --> 00:29:56,880
a four-dimensional diagram?

246
00:29:56,880 --> 00:29:57,760
Interesting question.

247
00:29:57,760 --> 00:30:01,320
I won't resolve it here, but it's suspicious.

248
00:30:01,320 --> 00:30:04,560
OK, now an interesting thing about these normal forms

249
00:30:04,560 --> 00:30:06,440
that we're going to see is that there's often

250
00:30:06,440 --> 00:30:09,240
going to be irrelevant information contained

251
00:30:09,240 --> 00:30:10,920
in the grid.

252
00:30:10,920 --> 00:30:13,320
Why put in irrelevant information?

253
00:30:13,320 --> 00:30:15,840
Because mathematicians are into this stuff,

254
00:30:15,840 --> 00:30:18,200
and mathematicians love completeness.

255
00:30:18,200 --> 00:30:20,920
Mathematicians like to cover every possibility.

256
00:30:20,920 --> 00:30:22,760
It's like, what would we do if we randomly

257
00:30:22,760 --> 00:30:24,160
pick an irrational number?

258
00:30:24,160 --> 00:30:25,680
Don't we have a proof that only that happens

259
00:30:25,680 --> 00:30:26,760
with zero probability?

260
00:30:26,760 --> 00:30:29,800
Yes, but what's our contingency plan for this event?

261
00:30:29,800 --> 00:30:32,240
All right, so for the zero probability event,

262
00:30:32,240 --> 00:30:34,080
I still need to know what we would

263
00:30:34,080 --> 00:30:37,000
do if the thing that never happens happened.

264
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:39,440
And you may say, who cares?

265
00:30:39,440 --> 00:30:40,400
I'm a mathematician.

266
00:30:40,400 --> 00:30:44,200
I care about what we do in every conceivable case.

267
00:30:44,200 --> 00:30:47,200
I don't care if the probability is zero or not.

268
00:30:47,200 --> 00:30:50,680
Anyway, so we can do a normal form of that one-player career

269
00:30:50,680 --> 00:30:54,280
game, which I will not transcribe because it's so simple.

270
00:30:54,280 --> 00:30:56,040
So again, it could be that if you drop out

271
00:30:56,040 --> 00:30:57,720
of high school, you get a pay-off of 10.

272
00:30:57,720 --> 00:30:59,600
Finish high school and then stop, you get 15.

273
00:30:59,600 --> 00:31:01,800
Finish a bachelor's degree and stop, you get 20.

274
00:31:01,800 --> 00:31:04,240
Finish a PhD and then stop, you get 30.

275
00:31:04,240 --> 00:31:07,440
Or finish two PhDs and stop, and you get a pay-off of zero.

276
00:31:07,440 --> 00:31:09,040
And you look at that and say, ah.

277
00:31:09,040 --> 00:31:10,120
So that's listed.

278
00:31:10,120 --> 00:31:12,640
Every payoff for every possible thing we'll have to do.

279
00:31:12,640 --> 00:31:14,840
And of course, people will do what you guys are doing,

280
00:31:14,840 --> 00:31:16,640
which is getting one PhD.

281
00:31:16,640 --> 00:31:19,480
That is exactly what all people should do, right?

282
00:31:19,480 --> 00:31:20,280
OK, good.

283
00:31:20,280 --> 00:31:22,780
Now, if we were to do a normal form of the French connection

284
00:31:22,780 --> 00:31:27,400
to the game, so then here, now we learn something

285
00:31:27,400 --> 00:31:29,440
that you can screw up.

286
00:31:29,440 --> 00:31:31,200
Even famous people screw up occasionally

287
00:31:31,200 --> 00:31:34,240
because it's like, wait, where do we put player one again?

288
00:31:34,240 --> 00:31:35,720
Where do we put player two?

289
00:31:35,720 --> 00:31:39,920
So the answer is player one goes in the front, goes on the left,

290
00:31:39,920 --> 00:31:41,480
and player two goes on the top.

291
00:31:41,480 --> 00:31:42,920
That's the way that we always do it.

292
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:49,600
So it looks like this.

293
00:31:49,600 --> 00:31:54,200
Then we've got on, off, on, off.

294
