00:42:30,000 --> 00:42:33,000
And if neither of you confesses, then you both get a light sentence.

45
00:42:33,000 --> 00:42:35,000
But of course if you both confess, then ha ha,

46
00:42:35,000 --> 00:42:37,000
then you're both going to get a pretty bad sentence.

47
00:42:37,000 --> 00:42:41,000
The worst one, though, is if you don't confess and your opponent does,

48
00:42:41,000 --> 00:42:43,000
then you get the really harsh punishment.

49
00:42:43,000 --> 00:42:45,000
So that's what the Prisoner's Lemma is all about.

50
00:42:45,000 --> 00:42:49,000
And the standard setup of the Prisoner's Lemma is people say, well, look,

51
00:42:49,000 --> 00:42:52,000
all right, well, can I control what my accomplice in the other room does?

52
00:42:52,000 --> 00:42:55,000
No. All right, so two possibilities.

53
00:42:55,000 --> 00:42:58,000
One is that he doesn't go and betray me.

54
00:42:58,000 --> 00:43:02,000
In this case, I could go and get off scot-free if I betray him,

55
00:43:02,000 --> 00:43:04,000
so that seems like a good idea.

56
00:43:04,000 --> 00:43:06,000
The other one is, well, maybe he is going to betray me.

57
00:43:06,000 --> 00:43:10,000
In that case, if I betray him, then at least I don't get the really bad sentence.

58
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:13,000
So that means that whether your opponent is betraying you

59
00:43:13,000 --> 00:43:18,000
or cooperating with you, you are always better off strictly by betraying him.

60
00:43:18,000 --> 00:43:20,000
That is the case.

61
00:43:20,000 --> 00:43:23,000
And then the nice thing about the Prisoner's Lemma is your accomplice

62
00:43:23,000 --> 00:43:25,000
has exactly the same incentives.

63
00:43:25,000 --> 00:43:27,000
So the other player has exactly the same incentives.

64
00:43:27,000 --> 00:43:30,000
So since it's strictly dominant for you to never cooperate with him,

65
00:43:30,000 --> 00:43:33,000
it's strictly dominant for him to never cooperate with you,

66
00:43:33,000 --> 00:43:35,000
and the game is dominant-solvable,

67
00:43:35,000 --> 00:43:39,000
and the dominant-solvable result is no one ever cooperates with anyone.

68
00:43:39,000 --> 00:43:43,000
And there is actually an economic detective model called Murder of the Martian

69
00:43:43,000 --> 00:43:47,000
you may have heard about, written by Ken Elzinga from UVA

70
00:43:47,000 --> 00:43:50,000
under the pseudonym Marshall Jevons.

71
00:43:50,000 --> 00:43:54,000
And in the detective novel, there's a guy who goes and confesses to a crime

72
00:43:54,000 --> 00:43:57,000
for which he's innocent, and the economist comes along and says,

73
00:43:57,000 --> 00:44:00,000
well, why would he confess if he's innocent?

74
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:02,000
And the economist says, have you ever heard of the Prisoner's Lemma?

75
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:04,000
Why don't we go explain?

76
00:44:04,000 --> 00:44:07,000
Of course, when you read the book, it's like, well, if that's true,

