00:46:07,000 --> 00:46:09,000
All right, so, next point.

117
00:46:09,000 --> 00:46:11,000
So if all players think like this,

118
00:46:11,000 --> 00:46:13,000
if all players sit around saying,

119
00:46:13,000 --> 00:46:16,000
I'm not going to play things that are always bad for me,

120
00:46:16,000 --> 00:46:21,000
and if other people know that other people are doing exactly the same reasoning,

121
00:46:21,000 --> 00:46:27,000
then you can actually often solve games that seem like they can't be solved at first glance.

122
00:46:27,000 --> 00:46:34,000
And this might actually lead to a situation where you are, like, untying a knot.

123
00:46:34,000 --> 00:46:37,000
Like, you're untying a knot, and how do we ever get through this?

124
00:46:37,000 --> 00:46:39,000
But then you untie one thing, and you're like,

125
00:46:39,000 --> 00:46:42,000
well, now that I've untied one thing, I see another thing I could do.

126
00:46:42,000 --> 00:46:45,000
And now that I've done that, pretty soon it's like, oh, my God, I've solved the problem.

127
00:46:45,000 --> 00:46:47,000
So that's an optimistic scenario, right?

128
00:46:47,000 --> 00:46:49,000
Sometimes it just gets worse and worse.

129
00:46:49,000 --> 00:46:54,000
But anyway, that's the idea, is that there are some games where once you eliminate something,

130
00:46:54,000 --> 00:46:56,000
then it turns out you can eliminate more.

131
00:46:56,000 --> 00:46:59,000
And once you eliminate that, you can eliminate more and more and more and more.

132
00:46:59,000 --> 00:47:02,000
So maybe the game that didn't look dominant solvable is.

133
00:47:02,000 --> 00:47:06,000
And then, yes, as you were questioning, a fun fact here is the order of iteration does not matter.

134
00:47:06,000 --> 00:47:10,000
It would probably matter when you're fixing really naughty shoelaces.

135
00:47:10,000 --> 00:47:14,000
But in game theory, it actually does not matter as long as you don't describe dominance.

136
00:47:14,000 --> 00:47:19,000
You can eliminate one thing that will not then, you know, whichever path you take,

137
00:47:19,000 --> 00:47:21,000
you're going to get to the same answer either way.

138
00:47:21,000 --> 00:47:24,000
Oh, no, that was just, yeah, go.

139
00:47:24,000 --> 00:47:25,000
Yes. All right.

140
00:47:25,000 --> 00:47:28,000
So here is a dominant solvable normal form for preps.

141
00:47:28,000 --> 00:47:30,000
All right. Let's see.

142
00:47:30,000 --> 00:47:33,000
And why don't I just, I'm not going to copy it.

143
00:47:33,000 --> 00:47:34,000
I'll just go and look at it.

144
00:47:34,000 --> 00:47:35,000
So we take a look here.

145
00:47:35,000 --> 00:47:37,000
Player 1 has two choices, S1 and S2.

146
00:47:37,000 --> 00:47:42,000
And Player 2 has three choices, T1, T2, T3.

147
00:47:42,000 --> 00:47:47,000
And when you go and take a look at it, say, well, let's see, for Player 1,

148
00:47:47,000 --> 00:47:51,000
neither S1 nor S2 is strictly dominant.

149
00:47:51,000 --> 00:47:57,000
Because S2 is better in some situations and S1 is better in other situations.

150
00:47:57,000 --> 00:48:05,000
But then you go over and look at the other one, and you can say here, well, so let's see.

151
00:48:05,000 --> 00:48:13,000
First of all, you could say that, yeah, so let's see, T3 is always worse than T2, right?

152
00:48:13,000 --> 00:48:19,000
So because, you know, so like rather, you know, so if Player 1 plays S1 and you do T2 instead of T3,

153
00:48:19,000 --> 00:48:20,000
then you get 7 instead of 4.

154
00:48:20,000 --> 00:48:21,000
That's better.

155
00:48:21,000 --> 00:48:24,000
And if Player 2 does S2, then you get negative 1 instead of negative 2.

156
00:48:24,000 --> 00:48:25,000
That's better.

157
00:48:25,000 --> 00:48:28,000
So you can lop off T3, right?

158
00:48:28,000 --> 00:48:31,000
So you can act as if T3 doesn't exist.

159
00:48:31,000 --> 00:48:38,000
And now, suddenly, we can take a look and we can see that S2 is actually strictly dominant, right?

160
00:48:38,000 --> 00:48:44,000
So why, so once you get rid of T3 as a possibility, then why should Player 1 ever play S1?

161
00:48:44,000 --> 00:48:46,000
So then he'll definitely play S2.

162
00:48:46,000 --> 00:48:50,000
And once you do that, then you can say why would Player 2 ever play T2 when he can do T1?

163
00:48:50,000 --> 00:48:53,000
And you can get 5 instead of negative 1.

164
00:48:53,000 --> 00:49:01,000
So it turns out then that 5, 5 is actually the only equilibrium in this game when we apply strict dominance.

165
00:49:01,000 --> 00:49:02,000
Make sense?

166
00:49:02,000 --> 00:49:08,000
So step one is we lop off T3, then we lop off S1, then we lop off T2, and then we're done.

167
00:49:08,000 --> 00:49:10,000
And we've narrowed it down.

168
00:49:10,000 --> 00:49:17,000
Even though at first it might seem like this is hopeless and we're not going to be able to use strict dominance at all this point.

169
00:49:17,000 --> 00:49:18,000
Okay.

170
00:49:18,000 --> 00:49:22,000
All right, now we come to a refinement.

171
00:49:22,000 --> 00:49:28,000
And this says if a probabilistic combination strategy strictly dominant is another for any probability distribution,

172
00:49:28,000 --> 00:49:34,000
and that last phrase is crucial, it's got to be for any probability distribution, right, then that too may be eliminated.

173
00:49:34,000 --> 00:49:37,000
And then this normal form again from Krabs becomes non-solvable.

174
00:49:37,000 --> 00:49:39,000
So what does that mean?

175
00:49:39,000 --> 00:49:42,000
It means, look, tell me any probabilities whatsoever that you might have,

176
00:49:42,000 --> 00:49:46,000
I'll tell you something better you can do than doing this thing.

177
00:49:46,000 --> 00:49:47,000
All right.

178
00:49:47,000 --> 00:49:49,000
So it's not just for one person's probabilities.

179
00:49:49,000 --> 00:49:52,000
It's got to be for any conceivable probabilities of something better.

180
00:49:52,000 --> 00:49:53,000
All right.

181
00:49:53,000 --> 00:49:54,000
So take a look at this.

182
00:49:54,000 --> 00:49:55,000
All right.

183
00:49:55,000 --> 00:49:56,000
So again, here we've got S1 and S2.

184
00:49:56,000 --> 00:49:58,000
We've got T1, T2, T3.

185
00:49:58,000 --> 00:50:00,000
Right.

186
00:50:00,000 --> 00:50:07,000
And at first you take a look and you say, well, you know, T3 can be better than T1 or T2.

187
00:50:07,000 --> 00:50:12,000
But then you can say, look, just tell me what probability you think the player one is going to,

188
00:50:12,000 --> 00:50:15,000
what probability do you think they're going to play S1 versus S2?

189
00:50:15,000 --> 00:50:22,000
And so suppose you say, well, fine, S1 they're going to play with probability greater than.5.

190
00:50:22,000 --> 00:50:27,000
In that case, you could say, well, then instead of doing T3, you should do T1 because on average that pays better.

191
00:50:27,000 --> 00:50:31,000
On the other hand, if you said, no, no, no, I think that they're going to play S2 with greater than.5 probability,

192
00:50:31,000 --> 00:50:34,000
then you say, well, fine, then I'll play T2 instead of T3.

193
00:50:34,000 --> 00:50:37,000
And you say, well, how about what if they're equal?

194
00:50:37,000 --> 00:50:41,000
Then say, look, either T1 or T2 would pay better than T3.

195
00:50:41,000 --> 00:50:46,000
So that's what allows you to get rid of T3 in this case.

196
00:50:46,000 --> 00:50:52,000
And then once we get rid of T3, then again this cleans up really nicely because then we can say,

197
00:50:52,000 --> 00:50:58,000
well, once we got rid of T3, then what is it that player one, then S1 is always better for player one.

198
00:50:58,000 --> 00:51:04,000
So then, and then if player one is always doing S1, then player two will definitely want to do T1.

199
00:51:04,000 --> 00:51:09,000
And again, we've unraveled the knot.

200
00:51:09,000 --> 00:51:11,000
All right.

201
00:51:11,000 --> 00:51:20,000
Now, it sometimes happens, may happen, that one strategy is strictly worse and sometimes strictly worse and never strictly better.

202
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:21,000
All right.

203
00:51:21,000 --> 00:51:25,000
In that case, we have another criteria.

204
00:51:25,000 --> 00:51:31,000
This is called weak dominance.

205
00:51:31,000 --> 00:51:39,000
And, you know, like an example of this might be going and sending someone that, you know, whose help you need an insulting e-mail.

206
00:51:39,000 --> 00:51:42,000
And then you say, look, one possibility is they'll remember and they'll hate me.

207
00:51:42,000 --> 00:51:46,000
Another possibility is they'll forget and nothing will ever happen.

208
00:51:46,000 --> 00:51:51,000
It's like, so the best case scenario is you don't hurt yourself.

209
00:51:51,000 --> 00:51:55,000
Say, yes, well, then common sense would say don't do that.

210
00:51:55,000 --> 00:52:00,000
Like either it's going to hurt you or there's no help whatsoever, so don't do it.

211
00:52:00,000 --> 00:52:01,000
That makes sense.

212
00:52:01,000 --> 00:52:10,000
Have you often wanted to told someone, look, best case scenario is this accomplishes nothing and otherwise you've made yourself worse off, so do not do this thing.

213
00:52:10,000 --> 00:52:12,000
I beg of you.

214
00:52:12,000 --> 00:52:13,000
All right.

215
00:52:13,000 --> 00:52:17,000
If you pleaded with someone like this, you have applied the concept of weak dominance.

216
00:52:17,000 --> 00:52:19,000
So this is another way of doing it.

217
00:52:19,000 --> 00:52:23,000
Unfortunately, when you do weak dominance, the order of iteration may matter.

218
00:52:23,000 --> 00:52:27,000
So then we actually wind up getting different predictions depending upon how we did it.

219
00:52:27,000 --> 00:52:32,000
So that's kind of weird, but that's the math, so I'm just a messenger here.

220
00:52:32,000 --> 00:52:44,000
So again, it seems like a really promising solution concept, except it's one that gives you totally different, can give you totally different answers depending upon which of the steps of elimination.

221
00:52:44,000 --> 00:52:45,000
All right.

222
00:52:45,000 --> 00:52:46,000
Questions?

223
00:52:46,000 --> 00:52:47,000
Questions?

224
00:52:47,000 --> 00:52:49,000
All right.

225
00:52:49,000 --> 00:52:50,000
Okay.

226
00:52:50,000 --> 00:52:57,000
So now we're going to move into a closely related solution concept of backwards induction.

227
00:52:57,000 --> 00:53:05,000
So in any game where there's perfect information, you say that each node marks the beginning, well, you're going to think of it as another game.

228
00:53:05,000 --> 00:53:10,000
So like right here, we can say that once we're here, we can just think of that as a totally new game.

229
00:53:10,000 --> 00:53:14,000
Once we're there, we can say, all right, fine, that's a totally new game.

230
00:53:14,000 --> 00:53:17,000
So that's just a way of looking at things.

231
00:53:17,000 --> 00:53:18,000
All right.

232
00:53:18,000 --> 00:53:24,000
So what happens if we repeatedly apply strict dominance to each of these sub-games?

233
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:33,000
So basically if we go to each sub-part of the tree and say, let's assume that whoever's making the choice applies strict dominance, and then we work our way back.

234
00:53:33,000 --> 00:53:43,000
So once we go to every end part and we apply strict dominance, then we go up a level and we apply strict dominance, then we go up a level and apply strict dominance as far as we possibly can.

235
00:53:43,000 --> 00:53:45,000
And what happens?

236
00:53:45,000 --> 00:53:49,000
All right, so again, the intuition is we want to very systematically reason.

237
00:53:49,000 --> 00:53:52,000
If we got to this point, no one would ever do these things.

238
00:53:52,000 --> 00:53:53,000
So let's erase them.

239
00:53:53,000 --> 00:53:56,000
And they don't have, they might just say it will never happen.

240
00:53:56,000 --> 00:54:02,000
And then once you've eliminated a bunch of things that will never happen, then it may be that you can actually figure out what's going on.

241
00:54:02,000 --> 00:54:09,000
But again, this is a lot like solving a hard math problem where if you can say, well, it starts off like, my God, I don't even know how to solve this thing.

242
00:54:09,000 --> 00:54:13,000
Like A could be greater than 1, equal to 1, less than 1.

243
00:54:13,000 --> 00:54:18,000
But then if you go and say, oh, wait a second, it's not possible for A to ever be less than 1.

244
00:54:18,000 --> 00:54:26,000
And then you say, okay, I'm going to throw out all the parts of the problem that never happen, and I'm only going to solve it for the part that does happen, and then I'm going to see if I can solve it.

245
00:54:26,000 --> 00:54:31,000
You may notice sometimes when you simplify a problem like that that it becomes easy.

246
00:54:31,000 --> 00:54:35,000
Okay, so the modest answer is we can eliminate more possibilities than before.

247
00:54:35,000 --> 00:54:37,000
All right, so here we go.

248
00:54:37,000 --> 00:54:40,000
So we got extensive and normal forms of preps.

249
00:54:40,000 --> 00:54:43,000
So here I'm only actually showing the extensive form.

250
00:54:43,000 --> 00:54:47,000
All right, so this is called the centipede game, the Rosenthal centipede game.

251
00:54:47,000 --> 00:54:53,000
All right, so if you take a look at this game, it looks like a centipede has got 100 nodes in it, or maybe 101, I can't remember.

252
00:54:53,000 --> 00:54:55,000
I think it is 100.

253
00:54:55,000 --> 00:54:57,000
Anyway, close enough.

254
00:54:57,000 --> 00:54:59,000
Approximately 100 nodes, like a centipede.

255
00:54:59,000 --> 00:55:04,000
All right, and the way that it works is in the last turn, both players get 100.

256
00:55:04,000 --> 00:55:09,000
But the whole idea of the game is you alternate deciding whether to end the game or not.

257
00:55:09,000 --> 00:55:15,000
And the way the payoffs are set up is that it can be advantageous for you to end the game early.

258
00:55:15,000 --> 00:55:21,000
So if you just take a look at the second to last turn, the second to last turn, player 2 is deciding,

259
00:55:21,000 --> 00:55:25,000
and if he ends it early, then instead of 100, he gets 101.

260
00:55:25,000 --> 00:55:30,000
So once you're at the second to last turn, what is it that player 2 wants to do?

261
00:55:30,000 --> 00:55:32,000
End the game. He said, I don't want to go to the last turn.

262
00:55:32,000 --> 00:55:36,000
I want to end it in turn, and I'm going to turn at minus 1.

263
00:55:36,000 --> 00:55:40,000
But if player 1 knows that player 2 is going to want to end the game a turn early,

264
00:55:40,000 --> 00:55:46,000
player 1 wants to go and end it two turns early, because then he'll get 99 instead of 98.

265
00:55:46,000 --> 00:55:50,000
But in that case, if player 2 knows that's going to happen, he wants to end it three turns early.

266
00:55:50,000 --> 00:55:53,000
Then player 1 wants to end it four turns early, and so on.

267
00:55:53,000 --> 00:56:01,000
We can reason backwards like this, and we get this bizarre result that actually,

268
00:56:01,000 --> 00:56:09,000
despite all of the advantages of cooperation, player 1 will immediately end the turn and get a payoff of 1 for both players.

269
00:56:09,000 --> 00:56:13,000
That is what comes of this backwards induction, where you say,

270
00:56:13,000 --> 00:56:16,000
look, if we ever got to that stage, here's what they would do.

271
00:56:16,000 --> 00:56:18,000
So what do I want to do at that point?

272
00:56:18,000 --> 00:56:20,000
What do you want to do at that point?

273
00:56:20,000 --> 00:56:23,000
And then, once you know, just work your way backwards.

274
00:56:23,000 --> 00:56:29,000
So the immodest answer is that if you've got any finite game of complete and perfect information,

275
00:56:29,000 --> 00:56:34,000
and there's no ties in the payoffs, then any game becomes dominant solvable.

276
00:56:34,000 --> 00:56:39,000
So remember, at first, dominant solvability sounds like something that hardly ever happened.

277
00:56:39,000 --> 00:56:43,000
And this says that as long as we've got perfect information, it always happens.

278
00:56:43,000 --> 00:56:45,000
What's the difference between complete and perfect information?

279
00:56:45,000 --> 00:56:48,000
It's a very picky distinction in game theory.

280
00:56:48,000 --> 00:56:49,000
I'm not going to worry about it.

281
00:56:49,000 --> 00:56:52,000
If you're really interested, you can go and Google it.

282
00:56:52,000 --> 00:56:59,000
Now, this implies, for example, that chess, which is a finite game of perfect information,

283
00:56:59,000 --> 00:57:01,000
it has a solution.

284
00:57:01,000 --> 00:57:03,000
There is an answer to chess.

285
00:57:03,000 --> 00:57:08,000
