So it's just gonna be English and whatever,

253
01:08:44,960 --> 01:08:46,200
whatever fears people have,

254
01:08:46,200 --> 01:08:48,360
it's not gonna be something else.

255
01:08:48,360 --> 01:08:49,200
Like so.

256
01:08:50,520 --> 01:08:51,360
All right.

257
01:08:51,360 --> 01:08:55,160
So that's the basics of Nash Equilibrium.

258
01:08:55,160 --> 01:08:56,880
Right, you know, so I got one other example

259
01:08:56,880 --> 01:08:57,720
of Nash Equilibrium.

260
01:08:57,720 --> 01:09:00,320
So when I was in fourth grade,

261
01:09:00,320 --> 01:09:03,080
there was a weird present exchange system,

262
01:09:03,080 --> 01:09:03,920
where first of all,

263
01:09:03,920 --> 01:09:06,360
every kid had to go and bring in a present,

264
01:09:06,360 --> 01:09:08,880
and then the first kid went and randomly picked a present,

265
01:09:08,880 --> 01:09:11,160
and the second kid goes and he randomly picks a present,

266
01:09:11,160 --> 01:09:12,760
and then he decides if he wants to get the present

267
01:09:12,760 --> 01:09:13,720
he randomly picked,

268
01:09:13,720 --> 01:09:17,200
or does he want to do a forced exchange with the first kid?

269
01:09:17,200 --> 01:09:18,040
Right?

270
01:09:18,040 --> 01:09:20,000
And then, you know,

271
01:09:20,000 --> 01:09:22,680
and then there's a rule that forbids the looping,

272
01:09:22,680 --> 01:09:25,160
so you aren't allowed to just cycle through.

273
01:09:25,160 --> 01:09:28,400
And, but anyway, so like as it proceeds,

274
01:09:28,400 --> 01:09:30,080
there's one present open that every kid wanted,

275
01:09:30,080 --> 01:09:32,160
which is magic sand.

276
01:09:32,160 --> 01:09:33,360
All right, this is a really fun toy,

277
01:09:33,360 --> 01:09:35,360
and I actually managed to somehow win this.

278
01:09:35,360 --> 01:09:36,680
But it's a magic sand,

279
01:09:36,680 --> 01:09:39,080
it's sand, you pour it into water, it holds its shape,

280
01:09:39,080 --> 01:09:40,120
and then if you drain it out,

281
01:09:40,120 --> 01:09:42,320
then it dries out, and then you can do it again.

282
01:09:42,320 --> 01:09:45,160
But anyway, this was the most popular present.

283
01:09:45,160 --> 01:09:47,240
So any time that a kid got another present,

284
01:09:47,240 --> 01:09:49,240
they would go and try to swap it out for the magic sand,

285
01:09:49,240 --> 01:09:51,320
and then whoever got the magic sand taken away from them

286
01:09:51,320 --> 01:09:54,040
would go and get another present, and so on.

287
01:09:54,040 --> 01:09:56,280
Right, and again, here's one where,

288
01:09:56,280 --> 01:09:58,280
at least it looked like there might not be any equilibrium,

289
01:09:58,280 --> 01:09:59,600
because people would just keep trying

290
01:09:59,600 --> 01:10:02,520
to go and get the preferred present by doing the swaps,

291
01:10:02,520 --> 01:10:04,480
and people would try to cycle through.

292
01:10:04,480 --> 01:10:07,840
I think the cycling was only prevented in one round,

293
01:10:07,840 --> 01:10:11,120
so it was possible to just cycle through.

294
01:10:11,120 --> 01:10:13,280
So in the end, I think that it didn't happen.

295
01:10:13,280 --> 01:10:14,440
Maybe the teacher tried to say,

296
01:10:14,440 --> 01:10:16,240
no, no, you can't go and take the magic sand,

297
01:10:16,240 --> 01:10:18,400
because you already had the magic sand once.

298
01:10:19,520 --> 01:10:22,680
All right, so now let's go and take a look

299
01:10:22,680 --> 01:10:25,400
at a few different examples.

300
01:10:25,400 --> 01:10:27,120
All right, so let's see.

301
01:10:27,120 --> 01:10:28,800
So let's start with this one here,

302
01:10:28,800 --> 01:10:30,240
where player one can do up or down,

303
01:10:30,240 --> 01:10:32,400
player two can do left or right.

304
01:10:32,400 --> 01:10:35,680
All right, and I will actually write this down.

305
01:10:35,680 --> 01:10:38,160
So we've got up or down,

306
01:10:39,680 --> 01:10:41,400
we've got left or right,

307
01:10:41,400 --> 01:10:43,400
and then we've got,

308
01:10:43,400 --> 01:10:45,720
say, pay us out to 15, 10,

309
01:10:45,720 --> 01:10:49,680
15, 10, seven, and six, eight.

310
01:10:49,680 --> 01:10:51,040
And how do we check for Nash equilibrium?

311
01:10:51,040 --> 01:10:53,160
We gotta go to every single equilibrium

312
01:10:53,160 --> 01:10:56,160
and see whether either player wants to switch

313
01:10:56,160 --> 01:10:58,560
or it costs what the other player's doing.

314
01:10:58,560 --> 01:11:00,320
All right, so we take a look here.

315
01:11:00,320 --> 01:11:02,400
All right, so if we're here,

316
01:11:02,400 --> 01:11:03,840
does player one wanna switch?

317
01:11:04,800 --> 01:11:07,080
No, it's holding constant what player two is doing.

318
01:11:07,080 --> 01:11:08,680
He would go and get 10 instead of 15,

319
01:11:08,680 --> 01:11:10,240
so he doesn't wanna switch.

320
01:11:10,240 --> 01:11:11,480
But how about player two?

321
01:11:11,480 --> 01:11:12,520
If player two were to switch

322
01:11:12,520 --> 01:11:13,880
holding constant what player one is doing,

323
01:11:13,880 --> 01:11:15,840
he would get 15 instead of 10, right?

324
01:11:15,840 --> 01:11:17,200
So that's not an,

325
01:11:17,200 --> 01:11:20,360
this cannot be a pure strategy Nash equilibrium,

326
01:11:20,360 --> 01:11:23,040
because one of the two players wants to switch.

327
01:11:23,040 --> 01:11:24,640
What if both players wanted to switch?

328
01:11:24,640 --> 01:11:27,840
Could that then be a pure strategy Nash equilibrium?

329
01:11:27,840 --> 01:11:31,160
No, if one or more people wants to switch,

330
01:11:31,160 --> 01:11:32,600
then it's not good.

331
01:11:32,600 --> 01:11:34,560
All right, now over here.

332
01:11:34,560 --> 01:11:36,360
So over here, you can say, let's see.

333
01:11:36,360 --> 01:11:38,160
So if we were here,

334
01:11:38,160 --> 01:11:40,240
then would player one wanna switch?

335
01:11:40,240 --> 01:11:41,920
And let's see, if player one were to switch,

336
01:11:41,920 --> 01:11:44,160
then he would get six instead of eight,

337
01:11:44,160 --> 01:11:45,720
so he does not wanna switch.

338
01:11:45,720 --> 01:11:46,720
If player two were to switch,

339
01:11:46,720 --> 01:11:48,040
then he would get 10 instead of 15,

340
01:11:48,040 --> 01:11:49,920
so that one looks good.

341
01:11:49,920 --> 01:11:51,240
All right, now we go over here.

342
01:11:51,240 --> 01:11:54,160
Again, why do we not stop once we find one?

343
01:11:54,160 --> 01:11:55,000
It could be multiple.

344
01:11:55,000 --> 01:11:57,040
Because there could be multiple, exactly.

345
01:11:57,040 --> 01:11:59,160
All right, totally possible.

346
01:11:59,160 --> 01:12:00,200
So we go over here,

347
01:12:00,200 --> 01:12:01,880
and we say, would player one wanna switch?

348
01:12:01,880 --> 01:12:03,480
Definitely, because he'd get 15 instead of 10.

349
01:12:03,480 --> 01:12:04,520
That's not good.

350
01:12:04,520 --> 01:12:05,640
And we're over here.

351
01:12:05,640 --> 01:12:06,920
We say, would player one wanna switch?

352
01:12:06,920 --> 01:12:08,680
Definitely, because he'd get eight instead of six.

353
01:12:08,680 --> 01:12:10,600
So then we're down to an equal.

354
01:12:10,600 --> 01:12:12,920
All right, now, so that's what we solved,

355
01:12:12,920 --> 01:12:16,200
what we solved for the pure strategy Nash equilibrium.

356
01:12:16,200 --> 01:12:18,440
Now, how does this differ from strict dominance?

357
01:12:18,440 --> 01:12:19,880
And let's see, I believe the answer

358
01:12:19,880 --> 01:12:21,280
to this problem is it doesn't.

359
01:12:21,280 --> 01:12:23,200
It's two different ways of getting the same answer,

360
01:12:23,200 --> 01:12:24,760
so let me just double check that.

361
01:12:24,760 --> 01:12:26,280
So let's see, player one.

362
01:12:26,280 --> 01:12:27,840
Yeah, so up is strictly dominant,

363
01:12:27,840 --> 01:12:30,320
because 15's better than 10, eight's better than six.

364
01:12:30,320 --> 01:12:32,680
So player one would never wanna move down.

365
01:12:32,680 --> 01:12:35,640
And then player two, let's see, 15's better than 10,

366
01:12:35,640 --> 01:12:36,640
so he'd never wanna do that.

367
01:12:36,640 --> 01:12:39,960
So yeah, so this brings us to a key truth,

368
01:12:39,960 --> 01:12:42,360
which is that if something is strictly dominant,

369
01:12:42,360 --> 01:12:45,000
then it's automatically the Nash equilibrium.

370
01:12:45,000 --> 01:12:47,600
All right, now, if you go take a break,

371
01:12:47,600 --> 01:12:51,240
and you can hand in homework right here,

372
01:12:51,240 --> 01:12:56,240
and then we will return in 10 minutes.

373
01:12:59,880 --> 01:13:01,160
Can you get the door, please?

374
01:13:01,160 --> 01:13:03,040
Sorry, hello?

375
01:13:03,040 --> 01:13:04,040
Door, door, please.

376
01:13:11,320 --> 01:13:14,840
All right, so we just went through one normal form

377
01:13:14,840 --> 01:13:19,840
and found, no, we just went through one normal form

378
01:13:19,840 --> 01:13:22,960
and found the pure strategy Nash equilibrium,

379
01:13:22,960 --> 01:13:24,200
and now we'll try another one.

380
01:13:24,200 --> 01:13:26,480
Now, here we've got the same possible strategies.

381
01:13:26,480 --> 01:13:28,240
Here we got up, down, left, right,

382
01:13:28,240 --> 01:13:30,160
and now the payoffs are different, right?

383
01:13:30,160 --> 01:13:32,120
So if you take a look at this one,

384
01:13:32,120 --> 01:13:34,520
here we can go and take a look,

385
01:13:34,520 --> 01:13:38,960
and so could up, left be an equilibrium?

386
01:13:38,960 --> 01:13:41,120
Then you will see no, that it's not possible,

387
01:13:41,120 --> 01:13:43,240
because player one would wanna switch to down,

388
01:13:43,240 --> 01:13:44,600
and player two would wanna switch to up,

389
01:13:44,600 --> 01:13:47,800
so it's not gonna be 10, no, it won't be 10, 10.

390
01:13:47,800 --> 01:13:51,960
Could, it's not gonna be up, left, could it be up, right?

391
01:13:51,960 --> 01:13:53,160
And you take a look at that, and you say,

392
01:13:53,160 --> 01:13:54,240
well, yeah, that's possible,

393
01:13:54,240 --> 01:13:56,400
because if player one switched,

394
01:13:56,400 --> 01:13:57,840
he would get negative five instead of zero,

395
01:13:57,840 --> 01:13:59,160
so that's worse for him,

396
01:13:59,160 --> 01:14:02,040
and if player two were to switch, let's see,

397
01:14:02,040 --> 01:14:03,360
then if player two were to switch,

398
01:14:03,360 --> 01:14:04,720
then he would get 10 instead of 15,

399
01:14:04,720 --> 01:14:08,600
so that's worse for him, so that's gonna be zero, 15,

400
01:14:08,600 --> 01:14:09,520
and now we're done, right,

401
01:14:09,520 --> 01:14:11,440
because we found the Nash equilibrium.

402
01:14:12,320 --> 01:14:15,000
Wrong, right, we have found one.

403
01:14:15,000 --> 01:14:16,560
There could be more, right?

404
01:14:16,560 --> 01:14:20,120
So then we move on to, to down, left,

405
01:14:20,120 --> 01:14:21,920
and there we see that we got 15, zero,

406
01:14:21,920 --> 01:14:23,880
and that turns out to be another one,

407
01:14:23,880 --> 01:14:26,120
and then finally, if we take a look at down, right,

408
01:14:26,120 --> 01:14:27,320
that's negative five, negative five,

409
01:14:27,320 --> 01:14:28,720
so that one's never gonna happen.

410
01:14:28,720 --> 01:14:31,920
So it turns out we've got two Nash equilibria there,

411
01:14:32,840 --> 01:14:33,840
two Nash equilibria.

412
01:14:35,120 --> 01:14:36,280
All right, oh yeah, I'm sorry,

413
01:14:36,280 --> 01:14:38,280
I forgot you had a question right at the end of break.

414
01:14:38,280 --> 01:14:39,560
Oh, it's not a problem.

