151
01:27:25,280 --> 01:27:29,000
And then he's going to get zero with probability one minus sigma.

152
01:27:29,000 --> 01:27:31,520
We want to find out when that's equal to

153
01:27:31,520 --> 01:27:34,000
the payoff that he gets from doing the other one, right?

154
01:27:34,000 --> 01:27:38,160
So, if it's right, then he's going to get 15 with probability sigma,

155
01:27:38,160 --> 01:27:42,080
but he's going to get negative five with probability one minus sigma.

156
01:27:42,080 --> 01:27:46,120
Okay. So, that's what it takes to make player two indifferent.

157
01:27:46,120 --> 01:27:48,720
Okay. Now, you've probably seen problems like this in

158
01:27:48,720 --> 01:27:51,240
algebra one and say, it's great.

159
01:27:51,240 --> 01:27:52,480
All right. And all it is,

160
01:27:52,480 --> 01:27:54,880
one equation, one unknown.

161
01:27:54,880 --> 01:27:58,120
So, what do we get? Sigma equals 0.5.

162
01:27:58,120 --> 01:28:00,520
Now, we're only half done. All right.

163
01:28:00,520 --> 01:28:02,320
So, now we need to go and figure out what does

164
01:28:02,320 --> 01:28:04,640
player two have to do to make player one indifferent.

165
01:28:04,640 --> 01:28:06,240
And for that to occur,

166
01:28:06,240 --> 01:28:11,520
let's go and say that five is going to be the probability of left,

167
01:28:11,520 --> 01:28:14,960
and then one minus five is the probability of right.

168
01:28:14,960 --> 01:28:18,520
Okay. So, now we go through here and we see that a player one,

169
01:28:18,520 --> 01:28:19,960
player one will be indifferent.

170
01:28:19,960 --> 01:28:22,520
So, he will be indifferent between playing left,

171
01:28:22,520 --> 01:28:24,760
and or rather, rather playing up and playing down,

172
01:28:24,760 --> 01:28:26,800
but eight times five,

173
01:28:26,800 --> 01:28:31,640
times five plus one times one minus five,

174
01:28:31,640 --> 01:28:39,840
equals 12 times five minus nine times one minus five.

175
01:28:41,480 --> 01:28:45,040
Okay. And again, if you're wondering where we get the payoffs for,

176
01:28:45,040 --> 01:28:46,560
so these first ones come,

177
01:28:46,560 --> 01:28:50,760
so these are the payoffs that you'll get from playing up.

178
01:28:50,760 --> 01:28:53,160
So, you get eight with probability five,

179
01:28:53,160 --> 01:28:55,280
and one with probability one minus five.

180
01:28:55,280 --> 01:28:59,400
All right. So, we're just reading off player one's payoffs from the first row.

181
01:28:59,400 --> 01:29:01,040
And then for the second part of the equation,

182
01:29:01,040 --> 01:29:04,200
we're reading off player one's payoffs from the second row.

183
01:29:04,200 --> 01:29:07,320
So, eight, one, 12, and negative nine.

184
01:29:07,320 --> 01:29:12,160
And we go through that, and we find out that five is going to be equal to five-sevenths.

185
01:29:12,160 --> 01:29:19,400
Therefore, the mixed strategy Nash equilibrium for this game will be

186
01:29:19,400 --> 01:29:27,400
sigma five equals 0.5 and five-sevenths.

187
01:29:27,400 --> 01:29:31,520
There we are. Okay. And here's the key point.

188
01:29:31,520 --> 01:29:35,440
If we're there, neither player will do better by changing their mix.

189
01:29:35,440 --> 01:29:37,960
Since each player has made the other one indifferent,

190
01:29:37,960 --> 01:29:41,000
neither one will be better off by changing.

191
01:29:41,000 --> 01:29:43,880
Now, would either be worse off by changing?

192
01:29:43,880 --> 01:29:48,440
Would either player be worse off if they unilaterally changed?

193
01:29:48,440 --> 01:29:50,320
No, they're indifferent.

194
01:29:50,320 --> 01:29:55,880
So, here is where it's key that Nash equilibrium just builds in.

195
01:29:55,880 --> 01:29:58,600
You have to have an affirmative incentive to deviate.

196
01:29:58,600 --> 01:29:59,880
You can't just be indifferent.

197
01:29:59,880 --> 01:30:03,200
So, this is set up where they are both indifferent.

198
01:30:03,200 --> 01:30:05,680
Right? And there it is.

199
01:30:05,680 --> 01:30:09,240
Now, a lot of people find mixed strategy Nash equilibrium totally bizarre.

200
01:30:09,240 --> 01:30:10,960
And it's usually like, well,

201
01:30:10,960 --> 01:30:13,800
why do people sit around trying to make other people indifferent?

202
01:30:13,800 --> 01:30:16,080
Has anyone ever said, you know what I want to do?

203
01:30:16,080 --> 01:30:17,520
Make my opponent indifferent.

204
01:30:17,520 --> 01:30:19,480
And the other guy says, wow, what a coincidence.

205
01:30:19,480 --> 01:30:22,400
I want to make you indifferent and you want to make me indifferent.

206
01:30:22,400 --> 01:30:24,360
So, how weird. All right.

207
01:30:24,360 --> 01:30:26,280
So, I say, you know, like this,

208
01:30:26,280 --> 01:30:28,560
that's really, really not a good way of thinking about it.

209
01:30:28,560 --> 01:30:33,000
I'd say the better thing about it is when you make your opponents indifferent,

210
01:30:33,000 --> 01:30:35,200
you're eliminating behavioral,

211
01:30:35,200 --> 01:30:37,760
you're eliminating behavioral patterns that they could exploit.

212
01:30:37,760 --> 01:30:40,360
Okay. So, think about applying this to sports.

213
01:30:40,360 --> 01:30:44,120
Suppose you always hit the ball to the far outfield.

214
01:30:44,120 --> 01:30:48,520
In that case, where should all of the people on the other team go?

215
01:30:49,240 --> 01:30:51,600
To the far outfield.

216
01:30:51,600 --> 01:30:54,400
Right? Like I remember when I was a kid and I was not much of an athlete.

217
01:30:54,400 --> 01:30:56,200
This probably surprised you a lot.

218
01:30:56,200 --> 01:30:58,240
Anyway, so when I come up,

219
01:30:58,240 --> 01:31:01,880
everyone crowds in because they know I'm not going to be able to hit the ball very far.

220
01:31:01,880 --> 01:31:06,000
All right. Now, I just wasn't actually able to hit it any further,

221
01:31:06,000 --> 01:31:10,400
at least without putting in more effort and initiative than I was going to do.

222
01:31:10,400 --> 01:31:14,000
So, but if I were able to go and vary where I was putting it,

223
01:31:14,000 --> 01:31:15,400
if I always put in the front,

224
01:31:15,400 --> 01:31:16,640
then people will go to the front,

225
01:31:16,640 --> 01:31:17,800
which means I'm likely to get out.

226
01:31:17,800 --> 01:31:20,400
So, I actually want to go and do the opposite of what they expect.

227
01:31:20,400 --> 01:31:25,920
But of course, if they know that you're trying to do the opposite of what you're expecting,

228
01:31:25,920 --> 01:31:28,680
then the natural thing for them to do is just to spread out.

229
01:31:28,680 --> 01:31:32,600
Right? And once you have spread the different things you're trying to do,

230
01:31:32,600 --> 01:31:35,640
and they have spread the different things that they're trying to do to counter you,

231
01:31:35,640 --> 01:31:38,760
then you have as an indirect result,

232
01:31:38,760 --> 01:31:40,280
each made each other indifferent.

233
01:31:40,280 --> 01:31:43,120
So again, the main goal is not really to wake up in the morning and say,

234
01:31:43,120 --> 01:31:44,600
how can I make other people indifferent?

235
01:31:44,600 --> 01:31:49,120
The main goal rather is to eliminate exploitable patterns in your behavior.

236
01:31:49,120 --> 01:31:52,000
And you can see this in a wide variety of sports,

237
01:31:52,000 --> 01:31:53,840
like anytime, any game like tennis,

238
01:31:53,840 --> 01:31:56,800
where there's a player that always hits the left,

239
01:31:56,800 --> 01:31:58,760
he's always on the left side of the court,

240
01:31:58,760 --> 01:32:01,040
you just sit on the left side of the court and the ball comes to you.

241
01:32:01,040 --> 01:32:03,440
Right? And then someone who's coaching that person says,

242
01:32:03,440 --> 01:32:05,200
stop always sending it to the left, you dummy.

243
01:32:05,200 --> 01:32:07,080
Go and randomize a bit,

244
01:32:07,080 --> 01:32:12,360
mix it up so that your opponent cannot just be sitting there to instantly return the ball.

245
01:32:12,360 --> 01:32:17,640
All right. So predictable player is easy to beat.

246
01:32:17,640 --> 01:32:20,760
Right? And of course the same goes in warfare.

247
01:32:20,760 --> 01:32:24,040
Right? Which again, has a lot of similarities to sports if you think about it.

248
01:32:24,040 --> 01:32:25,240
So if you always say,

249
01:32:25,240 --> 01:32:27,040
if you always do a frontal assault,

250
01:32:27,040 --> 01:32:29,920
then people can organize their forces to be ready for frontal assault,

251
01:32:29,920 --> 01:32:32,200
and then it's going to be highly ineffective.

252
01:32:32,200 --> 01:32:34,920
On the other hand, if you always wake them out,

253
01:32:34,920 --> 01:32:36,480
then they'll just be ready for you to always wake them up.

254
01:32:36,480 --> 01:32:37,720
But if you mix it up,

255
01:32:37,720 --> 01:32:40,160
then they're going to have to go and prepare for

256
01:32:40,160 --> 01:32:42,720
a wide variety of different strategies.

257
01:32:42,720 --> 01:32:44,680
They're going to burn up their resources,

258
01:32:44,680 --> 01:32:47,600
trying to deal with lots of different scenarios.

259
01:32:47,600 --> 01:32:50,760
And this actually is going to improve your prospects for winning,

260
01:32:50,760 --> 01:32:56,160
because they won't have been able to deploy the perfect strategy for whatever you do.

261
01:32:56,160 --> 01:32:57,920
Instead, they're going to have a bunch of

262
01:32:57,920 --> 01:33:00,440
imperfect strategies for a bunch of possibilities,

263
01:33:00,440 --> 01:33:02,640
and then you're going to wind up choosing one.

264
01:33:02,640 --> 01:33:06,840
Right? Or of course the same goes for strategy games.

265
01:33:06,840 --> 01:33:10,040
Right? So like if you always attack in the same place in a strategy game,

266
01:33:10,040 --> 01:33:12,320
then your opponents want to concentrate their forces right there,

267
01:33:12,320 --> 01:33:15,360
build fortresses and mines and everything else,

268
01:33:15,360 --> 01:33:16,880
and then you're not going to win.

269
01:33:16,880 --> 01:33:18,400
Whereas if you are unpredictable,

270
01:33:18,400 --> 01:33:20,360
