30
01:61:56,080 --> 01:62:02,640
So one, there's one equilibria, which is out-fight.

31
01:62:02,640 --> 01:62:06,960
This is one where the incumbent makes an empty threat to fight if the entrant enters, and

32
01:62:06,960 --> 01:62:10,840
then the entrant says, oh, I made an empty threat, I'm not entering.

33
01:62:10,840 --> 01:62:11,840
So that's one ending it.

34
01:62:11,840 --> 01:62:14,040
Why is this a Nash equilibrium?

35
01:62:14,040 --> 01:62:21,000
Because given that the entrant is staying out, is there any cost to the incumbent of

36
01:62:21,000 --> 01:62:22,000
making the empty threat?

37
01:62:22,000 --> 01:62:25,000
No, so like anything, there's no cost to anything.

38
01:62:25,000 --> 01:62:32,960
On the other hand, given that the incumbent has said, well, he has plans, I'm going to

39
01:62:32,960 --> 01:62:37,520
fight if you come in, then as long as the entrant believes the empty threat, does he

40
01:62:37,520 --> 01:62:38,520
want to enter?

41
01:62:38,520 --> 01:62:42,600
No, because if he stays out, he gets zero, it's a negative 10.

42
01:62:42,600 --> 01:62:47,640
So that is a Nash equilibrium where each one believes what the other, each one holds constant

43
01:62:47,640 --> 01:62:51,160
what the other one's plans are, then they want to stick to their plans.

44
01:62:51,160 --> 01:62:55,240
There is, of course, the other equilibrium, which is an accommodate.

45
01:62:55,240 --> 01:62:57,480
So that's a perfectly good equilibrium, too.

46
01:62:57,480 --> 01:63:01,920
So if the firm goes in, then the incumbent wants to accommodate, and if the entrant expects

47
01:63:01,920 --> 01:63:06,560
accommodation, then he definitely wants to go in.

48
01:63:06,560 --> 01:63:11,480
So these are two Nash equilibria, so they're both Nash equilibria, but only one is subgame

49
01:63:11,480 --> 01:63:12,480
perfect.

50
01:63:12,480 --> 01:63:13,480
How so?

51
01:63:13,480 --> 01:63:19,120
Well, the subgame perfect one requires to first go and figure out what will happen if

52
01:63:19,120 --> 01:63:21,320
this situation occurs.

53
01:63:21,320 --> 01:63:23,240
So if the entrant does play in, what will occur?

54
01:63:23,240 --> 01:63:26,720
Well, what will the incumbent actually do in that scenario?

55
01:63:26,720 --> 01:63:30,160
He'll accommodate, because he'd rather get four than negative 10.

56
01:63:30,160 --> 01:63:35,720
So in that case, we can just go and erase that as a viable possibility, and then the

57
01:63:35,720 --> 01:63:37,680
entrant isn't looking at the downside.

58
01:63:37,680 --> 01:63:40,680
Now the entrant's saying, I can have zero or four, what do I want?

59
01:63:40,680 --> 01:63:42,060
I'd rather have four.

60
01:63:42,060 --> 01:63:45,480
So in that case, the entrant's still saying, that's what I'm going to do.

61
01:63:45,480 --> 01:63:51,760
So this one is just a regular old Nash equilibrium, and this is a subgame perfect one.

62
01:63:51,760 --> 01:64:00,440
So they're both Nash equilibrium, but that is the one that rules out NP threats as having

63
01:64:00,440 --> 01:64:01,440
