01:71:30,240 --> 01:71:35,800
I think there's at least 100 living economists who owe their careers to this game.

210
01:71:35,800 --> 01:71:36,800
All right.

211
01:71:36,800 --> 01:71:39,440
Of course, you can't just write another paper on the same game.

212
01:71:39,440 --> 01:71:40,520
You have to tweak it a bit.

213
01:71:40,520 --> 01:71:44,520
But still, an enormous number of people have built their careers on this.

214
01:71:44,520 --> 01:71:49,280
And even to this day, you can still go and do experiments on the Prisoner's Dilemma and

215
01:71:49,280 --> 01:71:50,280
get tenure on that.

216
01:71:50,280 --> 01:71:53,840
Again, it takes a lot of originality to come up with an experiment nobody's done before,

217
01:71:53,840 --> 01:71:55,800
but it can be done.

218
01:71:55,800 --> 01:71:57,720
So here's the standard representation.

219
01:71:57,720 --> 01:72:01,880
So you've got two people who can either cooperate with each other or not.

220
01:72:01,880 --> 01:72:02,880
They decide this independently.

221
01:72:02,880 --> 01:72:07,440
If they both cooperate, then they both get a pretty good payoff of, say, six.

222
01:72:07,440 --> 01:72:11,480
If one cooperates and one doesn't, the non-cooperator does great.

223
01:72:11,480 --> 01:72:14,640
And the person who did cooperate gets a terrible payoff.

224
01:72:14,640 --> 01:72:18,960
And if they don't and neither cooperates, then they both get a pretty bad payoff.

225
01:72:18,960 --> 01:72:22,760
So the basic set of the Prisoner's Dilemma is that you both get the second best payoff

226
01:72:22,760 --> 01:72:24,120
if you cooperate.

227
01:72:24,120 --> 01:72:28,960
If you cooperate and the other guy does not, then you get the worst payoff.

228
01:72:28,960 --> 01:72:31,200
But he gets the best payoff.

229
01:72:31,200 --> 01:72:36,200
And if, on the other hand, you both fail to cooperate, then both of you get the third

230
01:72:36,200 --> 01:72:37,200
worst payoff.

231
01:72:37,200 --> 01:72:39,240
That's the setup.

232
01:72:39,240 --> 01:72:44,800
So when you're dealing with this, the natural solution to what people refer to is for dominance.

233
01:72:44,800 --> 01:72:48,480
Player one's better off not cooperating no matter what player two does.

234
01:72:48,480 --> 01:72:51,200
Player two's better off not cooperating no matter what player one does.

235
01:72:51,200 --> 01:72:55,080
Therefore, as night follows day, neither cooperates.

236
01:72:55,080 --> 01:73:01,120
At least this is what most theorists would tell you.

237
01:73:01,120 --> 01:73:05,240
Most theorists would at least know, oh, yeah, by the way, that's not what you really do

238
01:73:05,240 --> 01:73:06,240
in experiments.

239
01:73:06,240 --> 01:73:07,240
But then there's like, oh, what's going on?

240
01:73:07,240 --> 01:73:09,640
All right, so, all right.

241
01:73:09,640 --> 01:73:12,840
Now, the Prisoner's Dilemma has a lot of applications.

242
01:73:12,840 --> 01:73:17,120
And even though it fails experimentally, you can still say it's one of these models that

243
01:73:17,120 --> 01:73:20,080
